<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A shopkeeper has <m>{28}</m> kg of ordinary rice that cost Ksh <m>{145}</m> a kilogram and adds pishori rice that cost Ksh <m>{265}</m> a kilogram. The mixture is sold at Ksh <m>{234}</m> a kilogram, a profit of <m>{20}\%</m> on the cost.</p><p>(a) What did 1 kg of the mixture cost the shopkeeper?</p><p>Ksh <fillin characters="6" name="ans1"/>  </p><p>(b) How many kilograms of pishori rice were added? Give your answer exactly or correct to 1 decimal place.</p><p><fillin characters="6" name="ans2"/> kg  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) Profit is a percentage of the cost price: selling price <m>=</m> cost <m>\times {1.2}</m>. So the cost of 1 kg is <m>{234} \div {1.2} = {195}</m> shillings.</p><p>(b) Let <m>x</m> kg of pishori be added. Each kilogram of ordinary rice is <m>{195} - {145} = {50}</m> shillings below the mixture's cost and each kilogram of pishori is <m>{265} - {195} = {70}</m> shillings above it, so</p><p><me>{28} \times {50} = x \times {70}, \qquad x = \frac{ {1400} }{ {70} } = {20}</me></p><p>Check: <m>({28} \times {145} + {20} \times {265}) \div {48} = {195}</m>, and <m>{195} \times {1.2} = {234}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>195</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>20</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>